findings.suggestion:
Change `CryptoProvider.java:44` from `int order();` to:
```java
/**
 * Order of this provider. This allows to specify which CryptoProvider will have preference in case that more of them are on the classpath.
 * The higher number has preference over the lower number. Defaults to 0.
 */
default int order() {
    return 0;
}
```
This matches `ProviderFactory.order()` (server-spi/.../ProviderFactory.java:58), preserves binary compatibility for external implementers, and produces identical selection semantics in `CryptoIntegration.detectProvider` (un-updated providers get order=0 and lose to in-repo providers at order=200). The in-repo providers (`DefaultCryptoProvider`, `WildFlyElytronProvider`, `FIPS1402Provider`) and `AuthzClientCryptoProvider` already override `order()`, so their behavior is unchanged.

findings.body:
Lines 61-67:
```java
public Provider getBouncyCastleProvider() {
    try {
        return KeyStore.getInstance(KeyStore.getDefaultType()).getProvider();
    } catch (KeyStoreException e) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(e);
    }
}
```

This returns `KeyStore.getInstance("JKS").getProvider()` which is the default JDK security Provider (typically `SUN` on standard JDKs), NOT BouncyCastle. This violates BOTH the method name AND the interface javadoc (CryptoProvider.java:35: "BouncyCastle security provider. Can be either non-FIPS or FIPS based provider").

This is not a stub — it is an actively misleading implementation. Unlike the 15 methods that throw UnsupportedOperationException (which fail loudly), this method silently returns the wrong Provider. Any caller that branches on Provider identity (e.g. `if (provider.getName().startsWith("BC"))`) will take the wrong path. Any caller that uses BC-specific Provider Services (e.g. BC's X.509, PEM, or ECC implementations) may behave differently or fail with subtle errors.

getBouncyCastleProvider() has 44 callers across the codebase (common/util/PemUtils.java, common/util/KeyUtils.java, common/util/CertificateUtils.java, common/util/KeystoreUtil.java, common/util/BouncyIntegration.java, services/keys/*, services/x509/*, core/jose/jwe/*, core/jose/jwk/AbstractJWKParser.java). In authz-client standalone, only ECDSAAlgorithm is currently exercised (JWT ES256/384/512 verification), so this method is not reached at runtime today. But it is a latent trap: if any transitive code path in authz-client ever calls getBouncyCastleProvider() (e.g. via JWK parsing for EC keys, or PEM loading), it will silently receive SUN instead of BC.

The method is also internally inconsistent with the rest of the class: the 15 unsupported methods throw UnsupportedOperationException to signal partialness, but this method — which is equally unsupported in authz-client (no BC on classpath) — returns a wrong-but-valid Provider instead of failing loudly. This breaks the class's own stub convention.

Note: the method likely exists because getKeyStore() (line 198-200) needs a Provider indirectly, but KeyStore.getInstance(format.name()) uses the default provider anyway — the getBouncyCastleProvider() result is never wired into getKeyStore() in this class.

The 8-line r/s split preamble in concatenatedRSToASN1DER (lines 104-112) is byte-for-byte identical across all four ECDSACryptoProvider implementations:

```java
int len = signLength / 2;
int arraySize = len + 1;
byte[] r = new byte[arraySize];
byte[] s = new byte[arraySize];
System.arraycopy(signature, 0, r, 1, len);
System.arraycopy(signature, len, s, 1, len);
BigInteger rBigInteger = new BigInteger(r);
BigInteger sBigInteger = new BigInteger(s);
```

Locations: BCECDSACryptoProvider.java:29-36, BCFIPSECDSACryptoProvider.java:29-36, ElytronECDSACryptoProvider.java:37-44, AuthzClientCryptoProvider.java:104-112. The integerToBytes helper (lines 151-163) is likewise near-duplicated from BouncyCastle's X9IntegerConverter used by the other three providers.

The ECDSACryptoProvider interface (14 lines, 3 methods) has NO default methods and NO shared abstract base class. The only true variable across implementations is the ASN1 backend (BouncyCastle DERSequenceGenerator vs Elytron DEREncoder vs hand-rolled ASN1Encoder/Decoder).

The hand-rolled ASN1Encoder (101 lines) + ASN1Decoder (203 lines) = 304 new lines reimplementing DER encoding IS justified by the module constraint: authz-client must remain BouncyCastle-free and Java-8 compatible, so it cannot depend on BC or Elytron. But the r/s split preamble and integerToBytes byte-normalization are pure JDK (BigInteger + System.arraycopy) with no ASN1 dependency — they could be shared without violating the BC-free constraint.

A shared abstract base class `AbstractECDSACryptoProvider` in common/ providing the r/s split + integerToBytes helper, with abstract methods for the ASN1 backend only, would eliminate 4x duplication of the conversion core while preserving each module's ASN1 freedom.
